From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] neon: fix loadupdb reading past the end of the source on aarch64

loadupdb reads src[i >> 1], so a block of 1 << insn_shift output
elements needs only half as many source elements. The single-lane path
of the 64-bit loadupdb rule loads src->size << insn_shift bytes, twice
that, so the last block of a row reads up to half a block past the end
of the source array.

With loop_shift 2, e.g. video_orc_convert_I420_BGRA (4-byte
destination), the block at x = n - 4 loads 4 bytes at u + (n - 4) / 2,
2 bytes past the end of a chroma row of n / 2 bytes. If that row is the
last one of a buffer mapped with its exact size (e.g. a dma-buf from a
hardware decoder), this is a SIGSEGV.

Load only the bytes that are used. zip1 writes lane j of the loaded
register to output lanes 2j and 2j + 1, so output lanes
0 .. (1 << insn_shift) - 1 depend only on the lanes that are still
loaded, and the result is unchanged. The multi-register path
(size >= 16) loads 8-byte D registers, i.e. already size / 2 bytes,
and is left as is.
---
Patch-management notes, not part of the commit message.

  * Deliberately NO Signed-off-by: line.

  * Against orc 0.4.41, orc/orcrules-neon.c, the 64-bit branch of
    neon_rule_loadupdb(). Upstream split that file in 0.4.43; on main
    (0.4.44) the same code is orc_neon64_rule_loadupdb() in
    orc/orcrules-neon64.c, unchanged. Debian trixie's orc 1:0.4.41-1
    carries no Debian patch, so this hunk is the whole source difference
    between the liborc the image carries and Debian's.

  * UPSTREAM: not reported upstream as of 2026-09-26. loadupdb for
    aarch64 came with orc MR !53 (2020). Issue #32 / MR !72 fixed a
    different loadupdb discrepancy on 32-bit; no orc issue or MR matches
    this overread (GitLab API, 2026-09-26), and no release note up to
    0.4.44 mentions a fix. Drop this patch once the orc we build from
    carries one.

  * MEASURED on the
    6 GiB board, 2026-09-26: VE decode -> videoconvert -> kmssink,
    1920x1088 YV12 to BGRx, ORC's NEON JIT active (no ORC_CODE):
      Debian's liborc 1:0.4.41-1           4 of 10 to EOS, 6 SIGSEGV
      ORC_CODE=backup (the C fallback)    10 of 10 to EOS
      same-recipe build without this       5 of 10 to EOS
      same-recipe build with this         10 of 10 to EOS
    Two core dumps fault at ld1 {v0.s}[0], byte 958 of a 960-byte U
    row, 2 bytes before the end of a 3133440-byte mapping. The JIT code
    changes only in the loadupdb loads: in the I420->BGRA kernel the six
    ld1 {v0.s}[0] become ld1 {v0.h}[0]. Under qemu-aarch64 with source
    rows ending at a PROT_NONE page, 46 widths: 24-34 SIGSEGV per
    shift-1..3 kernel without the patch, none with it, and byte-identical
    output wherever the unpatched build survived. orc's own meson test
    passes 10/10 either way. It does run loadupdb at shift 2: exec_parse
    compiles every program of testsuite/test.orc, among them i420_to_ayuv
    and video_convert_orc_convert_I420_BGRA (4-byte destinations). It
    cannot see the overread, read in the 0.4.41 source: orc-test
    allocates each source row as wide as the destination (64-79
    elements), twice what loadupdb reads, so the extra bytes stay inside
    the row; every row also carries 256 bytes of padding and 16 rows
    above and below (EXTEND_STRIDE, EXTEND_ROWS in orc-test/orcarray.c),
    and its out-of-bounds check looks only at destination arrays.

  * FALSIFIER: the same command crashes with this liborc mapped
    (/proc/<pid>/maps names /usr/local/lib/aarch64-linux-gnu) and
    ORC_CODE unset.

  * NOT FIXED HERE, read in the code only. The 32-bit NEON rule loads
    size bytes too, but its odd-offset correction from !72 consumes one
    more source element, so its size cannot be halved mechanically. The
    aarch64 path lacks that correction for a destination not aligned to
    16 bytes; output is the same with and without this patch under qemu.
    Neither is the crash above.

  * Applies to orc 0.4.41 at --fuzz=0 (the build-orc stage, scripts/stages/orc.sh).

Assisted-by: Claude

--- a/orc/orcrules-neon.c
+++ b/orc/orcrules-neon.c
@@ -1139,15 +1139,20 @@ neon_rule_loadupdb (OrcCompiler *compile
       code |= (opcode&0xf) << 12;
     } else {
       /** load one single-element structure to one lane of one register */
+      /* 1 << insn_shift outputs read only half as many source elements
+       * (src[i>>1]); loading size bytes would read past the end of the
+       * source array on the last iteration */
+      int load_size = compiler->insn_shift > 0 ? size >> 1 : size;
+
       flag = 0;
-      if (size == 8) {
+      if (load_size == 8) {
         opcode = 4;
         flag = 1; /* size==01 */
-      } else if (size == 4) {
+      } else if (load_size == 4) {
         opcode = 4;
-      } else if (size == 2) {
+      } else if (load_size == 2) {
         opcode = 2;
-      } else if (size == 1) {
+      } else if (load_size == 1) {
         opcode = 0;
       } else {
         ORC_COMPILER_ERROR(compiler,"bad unaligned load size %d",
@@ -1155,7 +1160,7 @@ neon_rule_loadupdb (OrcCompiler *compile
         return;
       }
       ORC_ASM_CODE(compiler,"  ld1 { %s }[0], [%s]\n",
-          orc_neon64_reg_name_vector_single (compiler->tmpreg, size),
+          orc_neon64_reg_name_vector_single (compiler->tmpreg, load_size),
           orc_arm64_reg_name (ptr_reg, 64));
       code = 0x0d400000;
       code |= (opcode&0x7) << 13;
